    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "First Airmail Across the Pacific\nNovember 22, 1935 • Alameda, California, USA\nCHINA CLIPPER\nZAIRE LA CONQUETE DE L'AIR\n8K\nMACCHI - CASTOLD M.C. 72 1934\nSIKORSKI S-42 B (CLIPPER) 1934 - 1937\n22\n1983\n94501\nUSA\n20c",
      "International Tributes to the History of Flight\nFirst Airmail Across the Pacific\nThe historic journey of the \"China Clipper\" began as\nThe full-color cachet shows the \"China Clipper\"\nthe amplified voice of Pan American Airways Presi-\nhigh above the San Francisco-Oakland Bay Bridge on\ndent, Juan T. Trippe, reverberated above the Alameda\nits return flight.\nairbase, \"\n...the transpacific airway is ready to inau-\ngurate airmail service of the United States Post Ottice\nMint stamp: The mint stamp on this Cover was issued\nfrom the mainland, across the Pacific to the\nby Zaire in 1978. The aircraft in the foreground is the\nPhilippines...\"\nSikorsky S-42 which was the first long range \"Clip-\nOn November 22, 1935, the Martin M-130 flying\nper\" ship. It was used by Pan Am to survey the route\nultimately used by the \"China Clipper.\"\nboat, known as the \"China Clipper,\" began its jour-\nney. For the first time in history, the people on the\nPostmark: This Cover was postmarked in Alameda,\neast and west sides of the Pacific would be linked.\nCalifornia, the origination site of the \"China Clipper\"\nThis flight was a significant advance for Pan Am in its\nflight, on November 22,1983, the anniversary of the\neffort to build a commercial airline network.\nfirst airmail flight across the Pacific.\n© Postal Commemorative Society\nPrinted in U.S.A.\nW1G.0560.034"
    ],
